# Naming convention

Vocabularies by area live in `naming-supabase.mdc`, `naming-approvals.mdc`, `naming-cli.mdc` and `naming-adapters.mdc`; the Area table at the end points to the owning pages.

The names below are rules and part of the public API (invariant 14). The full inventory of exports, options, flags and reasons is on the pages in the table at the end; read the owning page before adding or renaming a public identifier, option, flag, reason or wire field.

- The brand is the noun. Type `PermDock`, instance `permdock`. `create*` sets up once at module scope, `get*` resolves per request on the server, `use*` reads on the client. Core and `permdock/pdp` build an instance with `getPermDock(policy, user)` (async) and `getPermDockSync` (throws when an input answers with a Promise); they export no `createPermDock`. Every factory that hands out instances is `createPermDock`: every server and agent adapter and the four feature entries `permdock/agent`, `permdock/workflow`, `permdock/delivery` and `permdock/chat`. Never a function that returns `X | Promise<X>`. Its return type is `<Entry>PermDock<V>` (`ServerPermDock`, `AgentPermDock`, `WorkflowPermDock`, `DeliveryPermDock`, `ChatPermDock`), and the import path names the entry (`permdock/next`, `permdock/hono`). A factory that returns a handler or a store instead of an instance is named after what it returns, without `create` (`evaluationsHandler`, `nativeStore`). Never `NextDock`, `HonoDock`, `dock`, `ability`, `can` as a definer, `create*Kernel`, or `$`-prefixed members.
- Instance access: `getPermDock(input)` is the instance getter on every factory result (`permdock/server`, `node`, `hono`, `express`, `fastify`, `elysia`, `nest`, `trpc`, `orpc`, `terminal`, `openai`, `claude-agent`, `eve`, and `WorkflowPermDock.getPermDock(ticket)`). Middleware is `middleware()` (`hono`, `express`, `elysia`, `trpc`, `orpc`), and a fastify plugin value is a function too. The mounted endpoint is `permdockHandler()`, always of the shape `() => <framework handler>`. Never a `permdock()` member.
- Methods and feature functions: the decisions every app makes are methods on the instance: `decideEach(permission, rows)` and `decideFor(principalId, permission, row)`; `decideFor` never widens the caller and never consumes a `limit`; `permittedIds`, `permittedScopeIds`, `mayUse`, `mayUseMany` and `filterIds` are methods too, and `where(permission, { tenant? })` takes the tenant to compile for. A feature's own request is a free function that takes the instance first, so a bundler drops the feature when an app does not import it: `decideCredential(permdock, request)`, `decideAutomation(permdock, request)`, `decideSupportSession(permdock, request)`, `decideRoleChange(permdock, change)`, `activate(permdock, input)`, `whoCan(permdock, permission, row)`, `simulateArazzo(permdock, plan)`. They read the instance's internals and deny with `unsupported` (or list nothing) on an instance built from a snapshot.
- Generics follow the instance: a factory that hands out an instance takes `Policy<TUser, TPrincipal, V>` and types it `PermDock<V>` everywhere it reaches user code (the `<Entry>PermDock<V>` return type, request and context types such as `ExpressPermDockRequest<…, V>`, `ConvexPermDockContext<TCtx, V>`, middleware results, `PdpPermDock<V>`). An adapter whose framework cannot carry a type into the handler offers a typed `withPermDock(handler)` wrapper instead of augmenting the framework's types. Each such adapter has a `tests/<adapter>/vocabulary.test-d.ts` over `packages/permdock/tests/fixtures/vocabulary.ts`. Adapters that hand out no instance (`ai-sdk`, `mcp`, `a2a`, `authzen`, `openapi`, `ssf`) and those whose framework erases it (`nest` parameter decorators, `trpc`'s `AnyTRPCMiddlewareFunction`) take `V` only through the policy argument.
- Row types: a leaf's row type is `RowOf<P>`, read from the type-only `PERMISSION_ROW` key. Every `data` a check takes is a `RowData<P>` (a `Partial` row or a `{ current, next }` pair), with an `UncheckedData<D>` overload for data typed `unknown` or `any`. Never `InferRow`, `DataOf` or a second row parameter on a check. Vocabulary helpers are `<Thing>Of<P>`: `PermissionOf`, `RoleNameOf`, `ScopeNameOf`, `PlanNameOf`, `OperationNameOf`, `VocabularyOf`; never `Infer<Thing>`.
- Subjects: every provider is `subjectFrom<Source>(input, options)`, returns `Subject<<Source>Principal>` and never throws (an unverifiable input is the anonymous subject); each exports its `<Source>Principal` type (`ApiKeyPrincipal`, `CapabilityPrincipal`, `IntrospectionPrincipal`) and takes a `schema` option. The input comes first and options second: `subjectFromApiKey(key, options)`, `subjectFromBetterAuth(session, { auth, … })`, `subjectFromEve` and `actorFromEve`. Subject builders inside the package are not exported, and an SSF subject lookup is an `SsfSubjectResolver`. Types come from `PrincipalOf` / `SubjectOf`; never `declare module` augmentation or a `$Infer` accessor. Where a provider reads a value from verified claims is its `claims` option, a `ClaimsMap` (`id`, `roles`, `groups`, `entitlements`, `plans`, `tenant`, `memberships`, `kind`, `assurance`, `session`; each a dot path or a function), never a top-level `roles` / `tenant` path or a provider-specific name such as `globalRoles`. The SQL side names one claim per setting (`rls.tenantClaim`, `rls.roleClaim`, `supabaseClaims({ tenantClaim })`), because the database reads a top-level claim by name.
- Principals: `PrincipalSource` is the one identity lookup, passed as `principals`: `principal(id, { tenant? })` and `principalByIdentity({ kind: 'email' | 'chat', provider?, value, workspace? })` return a live `Principal` or `null` (`memoryPrincipalSource`, `testPrincipalSource`). It replaces every `owner(id)` callback; never a `ChatIdentitySource`. A `kind: 'contact'` principal is an external person (a customer contact, a portal user) with only `{ on }` memberships; it never approves, publishes or creates keys, and is read-only by default.
- Operations: `defineOperations(permissions, { name: { permission, data?, results?, meta? } })` returns a frozen `OperationMap`, with `meta` `{ risk?, effects?, title? }`. Agent tools, workflow nodes, MCP operations, `protect(operation)`, oRPC's `operationsRouter(operations, routes, { meta? })` and chat commands read the same map, and the catalog lists it as `operations`.
- Scopes: `definePolicy({ scopes })` declares named scopes in order, each `{ key, within? }`, one tree rooted at the first; `tenant` / `team` alias the first two. A `Membership` is `{ scope, id, within }` or `{ on }`, never `{ tenant, team }` (removed in 0.17.0; `permdock codemod 0.17` rewrites it); a role applies only at its own scope (no cascade), and RLS helpers are `permitted_<scope>_ids(p_grant)` plus the membership-only `member_<scope>_ids()`, and on Supabase `member_<scope>_ids_for(p_user)` (executable by `supabase_auth_admin` only, granted next to the function, for `supabase.hook.claims` functions and better-supabase's `memberIdsFor`), `member_<scope>_ids_of(p_user)` for a scope named `team` or `teams` (executable by `authenticated`, no rows unless `p_user` is the caller, for better-supabase's `teamIdsOf`), whose `member_<scope>_ids_for` takes `(p_user, p_active text default null)`, the one signature `supabase.hook.teams` also writes as `member_team_ids_for` and `permdock_user_id()`, the one subject read of every generated helper and policy (`auth.uid()`, null for an empty `sub`); `permdock_role_grant_keys(p_scope, p_role)`, the one role-to-grant-key lookup every helper body calls with the `table` seeds (no client role may execute it); in `database` mode `permdock_has_for(p_user, p_grant)` and `permitted_<scope>_ids_for(p_user, p_grant)` answer for a named user and no client role may execute them, and `permitted_<scope>_users(p_permission, p_id)` lists who holds a permission in one instance, also executable by no client role; with `rls.readOnlyActors`, `permdock_actor_may_write()` is what hand-written write policies call; `permdock_has_permission(p_permission)`, `permitted_<scope>_ids_by_permission(p_permission)` and `grant_keys(p_permission, p_scope, p_effect)` take a permission key, never a `#n` grant key; `permdock_role_permissions(p_role, p_scope, p_tenant, p_scope_id)` and `permdock_permission_keys()` (or `(p_scope)` for the keys roles hold on one scope) read a role's keys and the catalog for role editors, executable by `authenticated`, and `permdock_trusted_role_permissions` reads them for server code, executable by no client role and by the `rls.trustedReaders` roles; `permitted_<scope>_permission_keys(p_id[, p_keys])` lists the keys held on one instance in one set-based query (`_for(p_user, p_id[, p_keys])` in `database` mode). Never a hard-coded tenant/team kind in new code.
- Graph: a resource's `parent` may name itself, `restricted` names a boolean column or `{ field, stops }` (`'parent'` and link names it closes; a closed link also checks the rows above through `permdock_restricted_<resource>`), and relations are `{ field, memberOf? }`, `{ edge, object?, subject?, expiresAt? }` or `{ principal, period? }`. `relation(resource, name, { through: 'parent', depth })` walks the chain (default 16, at most 32) through a `RelationSource` (`ancestors`, `related`; `memoryRelationSource`, `testRelationSource`) passed as `relations`, cached per instance; `prefetchRelations` fills the cache and `whoCan` lists holders with `complete`, never granting. Reasons `relation-depth` and `relation-unavailable`. Snapshots carry no graph (graph grants are `portable: false`). RLS: `permdock_closure` with statement triggers, `permitted_<resource>_ids(relation)` (resource in snake_case), `rls.rowHelpers` with `permitted_<resource>_rows(permission)`, `_rows_for(p_user, permission, p_claims)` and `_rows_for(p_user, permission, p_ids, p_claims)` `rls.idTypes` (the helpers' id type: `uuid` on `supabase`, `text` on `neon`), the row-valued `can_<resource>(p_row, p_permission)` and the per-id `can_<resource>(p_id, p_permission)`, both over `permitted_<resource>_match(p_row, p_permission)`, the set helpers `permitted_<scope>_id_array(p_grant)`, `permdock_<root scope>_has(p_grant, p_tenant)`, `permitted_<resource>_rows(p_permission, p_tenant)`, `permitted_<resource>_ids_in(p_permission, p_ids)` and `permitted_<resource>_count(p_permission, p_tenant, p_cap)`, `inherit(permission, { through })` as a grantee read through `RelationSource.row`, `rls verify --tree`, doctor PD031 / PD032 ([relationships](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/relationships.mdx)).
- Options, not exports: an option is named after its interface noun, and `store` means only `ApprovalStore`. The list: `webBotAuth`, `store` (`ApprovalStore`), `sink` (`DecisionSink`), `source` (`SnapshotSource`, on `permdock/react-native`), `policies` (`PolicySource`), `verifier` (`TokenVerifier` or `CredentialVerifier`), `signer` (`TokenSigner`), `memberships` (one `MembershipSource` or an array, composed), `customRoles` (`CustomRoleSource`), `subjects` (`SubjectCache`), `entitlements` (`EntitlementSource`), `settings` (`SettingsSource`), `relations` (`RelationSource`), `limits`, `revocations` (`RevocationFeed`), `approvalPolicies` (`ApprovalPolicySource`), `approvalDelegations` (`ApprovalDelegationSource`), `principals` (`PrincipalSource`), `automations` (`AutomationSource`), `waiter` (`ApprovalWaiter`), `directory` (`DirectoryStore`, on `scimHandler`) and `reviews` (`AccessReviewStore`). The decision call site is `call`, never `source`. Snapshot persistence on `permdock/react-native` is a `SnapshotStorage`.
- Defaults, runners, sources, verifiers: every in-process default is `memory<Interface>` (`memorySettingsSource`, `memoryRelationSource`, `memoryVersionCache`, `betterSupabaseVersionCache`, `memorySubjectCache`, `memoryDecisionSink`, `memoryApprovalPolicySource`, `memoryApprovalDelegationSource`, `memoryAccessReviewStore`, `memoryCredentialStore`, `memorySnapshotStorage`, `memoryPrincipalSource`). Every interface has a `test<Interface>` runner in `permdock/testing` (`testDecisionProvider`, `testSnapshotStorage`, `testPrincipalSource`, `testSubjectCache`), and a parity runner is `test*Parity` (`testRlsParity`, `testOrmParity`, `testClientParity`). A fixture helper names what it does: `snapshotFixture`, `seedRows(query, rows)`. An adapted source is `<origin><Interface>` (`stripeEntitlementSource`, `tableMembershipSource`, `junctionMembershipSource`, `tableApprovalDelegationSource`, `powerSyncSource`, plus `supabasePostgresQuery`); `fromSnapshot` keeps its name. A verifier factory is `<thing>Verifier(options)` (`webBotAuthVerifier`, `apiKeyVerifier`). Never a new `from*` source, a `verify*` factory, or a `memory*` name that is not an interface name.
- Hooks: one meaning per name. `onDenied` throws (core and `permdock/next`); `deniedResponse` builds the Problem Details response (server adapters); `deniedText` is the text a model reads (agent adapters); `onError` receives an error a check threw and never changes the denial (better-supabase's `authorizer` and `toolPolicy`, and `permdock/mcp`'s `protectServer`); `data` loads the row, as on an operation binding. Never `loadData`, `load`, `onFailure` or `reportError`.
- Brand casing: an acronym brand is one capital then lower case (`A2a`, `Ssf`, `Authzen`: `testAuthzen`, `AuthzenVectors`), and a camel-case brand keeps its capitals (`PowerSync`: `powerSyncSource`). Identifiers use the `-ize` spelling (`summarizeSubject`); prose stays British.
- Failure causes: a failure-cause type is `<Thing>FailureCause` and its field is `cause` (`WebhookFailureCause`, `ApprovalInspectResult.cause`); `reason` is kept for the closed denial list. Error codes have one spelling each, such as `approval-not-pending`.
- Definitions and policy: `definePermissions`, `resource`, `definePolicy`, `role`, `allow`, `deny` and the other plain functions on `concepts/policies.mdx`; `crud` / `readable` / `writable` are option factories for `resource()`. Never a fluent policy builder.
- App definition: `definePermDock(policy, options)` from `permdock/app` returns a policy with `getPermDock`, `fromClaims`, `fromApiKey`, `sources`, `evict` and `forClient` (bound by the `sources(client)` option). `defineApp` is removed, and the result has no `.next` or `.authorizer` member (`docs/decisions/0056-define-permdock-entry.md`).
- Errors: `PermDockDeniedError`, `PermDockApprovalRequiredError`, `PermDockValidationError` and `PermDockRevokedError`. Any other error class is a domain error (`ApprovalError`, `AccessReviewError`, `DirectoryUniquenessError`, `DirectoryNotFoundError`, `InvalidSignatureError`), listed on `getting-started/naming.mdx`, with an `is<Name>` guard (`isApprovalError`, `isAccessReviewError`). Web Bot Auth failures throw `InvalidSignatureError` and never become an anonymous actor.
- Open and closed unions. An id that core only compares for equality, where an unknown value can only fail to match, is an `OpenUnion` (`T | (string & {})`): `ActorKind`, `AdapterId`, `MembershipVia`, `DirectoryEvent.source`. A union whose members core interprets one by one stays closed: denial reasons, trace skip reasons, approval modes and statuses, condition operators, credential kinds, operation risks, delivery channels, problem types and preset ids.
- Denial reasons are a closed list (`concepts/decisions.mdx`). A boundary failure is `validation`, never `invalid-resource`; an undeclared role is `unknown-role`.
- Never `SnapshotV2` (the major is the `v` field), never `@permdock/cloud` (it is the `permdock/cloud` entry), never an entry for a tool PermDock only composes with, such as `permdock/hey-api`, `permdock/scalar` or `permdock/mintlify`. An entry is core, a feature contract (`permdock/agent`, `permdock/workflow`, `permdock/delivery`, `permdock/chat`), an implementation named after the npm package whose hooks it maps onto a contract (`permdock/ai-sdk`, `permdock/resend`), or `permdock/cloud`. A client sub-entry of an adapter (`permdock/next/client`, `permdock/supabase/client`) is not an entry of its own ([adapters](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/index.mdx)). `with*` belongs to the Supabase middleware pipeline, to `withSubject`, the transaction helper of `permdock/drizzle`, `permdock/kysely` and `permdock/prisma`, and to `withOtel`, the instance wrapper of `permdock/otel`. `permdock/terminal` is for consumers' CLIs and is not the `permdock` binary or `permdock/cli`; never a scoped `@permdock/*` package (`permdock`, owned by the `scaledockhq` npm organisation, is the only published name); the build hooks `createPermDockPlugin` / `createPermDockUnplugin` collect only.
- Renames: `definePermissions(tree, { renamed })` maps former keys to current ones; the catalog field is `renamedFrom`, the diff kinds are `renamed` and `alias-removed`. Never `aliases` or `legacyKeys`.
- Snapshots: `snapshotFor`, `assignableFor` (one tenant's `assignable` entry on demand) and `mayAccess` are the cache building blocks. PermDock never adds `'use cache'` or calls `headers()` / `cookies()` itself; `getSnapshot` on the `permdock/next` factory calls `cacheLife` and `cacheTag` only inside the app's own `'use cache: private'` scope; `tenants: 'all'` is opt-in and the default ships the active tenant's grants; `omitStatic` replaces the grants of declared roles with `static` references into the `ClientBundle` that `clientBundle` builds and `permdock generate` writes as `bundle.ts`, passed as the provider's `bundle` option, and `meta.clientBundle: false` keeps one role inline ([Next.js Cache Components](../../apps/docs/content/docs/guides/next-cache-components.mdx)).
- Ownership: role options `min`, `max`, `transferOnly`, `assigns`, `for` and `meta.audience` (`RoleMeta`); `permdock.decideRoleChange(change)` (`scope: 'global'` with no `id` for a role with no `on`; `assignableRoles({ scope: 'global' })` lists them) answers `granted` / `denied` with the closed reasons `last-holder`, `max-holders`, `transfer-only`, `not-assignable-by`, `self-demotion`, `not-allowed-for-membership`, `conflicting-role`, takes the actor from the instance and fails closed without `holders`; a role held through a kind outside `for` is dropped at resolution; an `assigns` graph replaces the held-grant rule for `assignableRoles` and ranks `heldRoles`; `audiences()` / `snapshot.audiences` never grant. SQL objects are `permdock_can_assign`, `permdock_holders_<scope>`, `permdock_transfer_only_<scope>_*` and the `rls.assignments` triggers `permdock_assignment_<table>` on the membership, global-roles and listed tables, with `ownRole: 'refuse'` for the caller's own rows, and `permdock_replace_global_roles(p_user, p_roles)` (invoker rights) over the `rls.roles` table; doctor PD026 and PD064 ([ownership](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/ownership.mdx)).

